#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506580 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147398 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f692eea3-1a29-5067-86dd-16a56be40703" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Total Change of a Function Given Its Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6f34e98-745c-508e-9aac-c7930cd0847b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "256" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506332}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6f34e98-745c-508e-9aac-c7930cd0847b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "256" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506332} :answer-field/id #uuid "bdb50e03-36f7-5d1f-9178-33250a489690" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506469}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b1c05acb-14b2-5a23-a0fb-57fef367611e" :question/math-academy-id "q-349973" :question/problem "The rate at which water flows into a tank is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=6t+10,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in liters per hour and $t>0$ is the time in hours since $1\\,\\text{p.m}.$\nWater is also draining from the tank at a steady rate of $8$ liters per hour.\n\nIf there were $200$ liters of water in the tank at $1\\,\\text{p.m},$ how many liters of water will be there at $5\\,\\text{p.m}?$\n\nThere will be \n{{field-1}}  liters of water in the tank at $5\\,\\text{p.m}.$" :question/worked-solution "Water is flowing into the tank at a rate of $6t+10$ liters per hour and is draining at a rate of $8$ liters per hour. Therefore, the **rate of change** in the amount of water (call it $R(t)$) is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =r(t)-8 \\\\  & =6t+10-8 \\\\  & =6t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the **total amount of water** after $t$ hours (call it $T(t)$), we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =T(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}R(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =200+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(6x+2)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that $T$ is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration. \n\nTo find out the amount of water after $4$ hours ($5\\,\\text{p.m}$), we substitute $t=4$ into the above and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(4) & =200+{\\int }_{0}^{4}(6x+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =200+(3{x}^{2}+2x){|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =200+[(3\\cdot 16+8)-0] \\\\  & =200+56 \\\\  & =256\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there will be $256$ liters of water in the tank at $5\\,\\text{p.m}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506554 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147398 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f692eea3-1a29-5067-86dd-16a56be40703" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Total Change of a Function Given Its Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9635b2b-705b-58e9-94bd-88166205012c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t^{2}-4t+100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506247}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9635b2b-705b-58e9-94bd-88166205012c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t^{2}-4t+100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506247} :answer-field/id #uuid "9eaf115f-1435-52c2-b951-5e9b2f69cb63" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506451}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7896667b-b339-512f-9dbb-66fd90855442" :question/math-academy-id "q-349984" :question/problem "A city is tracking the number of bikes available in a bike-share station.\nThey observe that bikes are returned to the station at a rate $r(t)$ given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=5{t}^{3}+2t,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in bikes per hour, and $t>0$ is the time in hours since tracking began.\nAt the same time, $4$ bikes are rented from the station each hour.\n\nIf there were initially $100$ bikes at the station, find the expression that gives $B(t),$ the number of bikes at the station $t$ hours after tracking began.\n\n$B(t)=\\frac{5}{4}{t}^{4}\\,+$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The number of bikes is increasing at a rate of $r(t)$ bikes per hour and is decreasing at a rate of $4$ bikes per hour. Therefore, the **rate of change** $R(t)$ of the number of bikes is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =r(t)-4 \\\\  & =5{t}^{3}+2t-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the total number $B(t)$ of bikes after $t$ hours, we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =B(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}R(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =100+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(5{x}^{3}+2x-4)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that our final function is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration.\n\nWe can simplify the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =100+{\\int }_{0}^{t}5{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{t}2x\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{t}4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =100+{5\\,\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{t}+{2\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{t}-4x{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =100+\\frac{5}{4}{t}^{4}+{t}^{2}-4t \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}{t}^{4}+\\,{t}^{2}-4t+100\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319359 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147399 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3798ec35-19ef-559f-b8f8-cb0a0a03157b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Global Minimum or Maximum of a Function Given Its Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ae082c6-947e-5375-a9f4-aa88253e382d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "188" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318032}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ae082c6-947e-5375-a9f4-aa88253e382d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "188" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318032} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cc1a0df-9be5-533e-b00c-ee8fecf41b26" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318510} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06774482-8185-522a-b0da-abe466cad0be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317591}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06774482-8185-522a-b0da-abe466cad0be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317591} :answer-field/id #uuid "57c75693-c5b3-5428-8192-d75e3b45beab" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318615}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8b0963b7-9111-575b-994d-33c581e812aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-349996" :question/problem "On a certain afternoon, the total number of people $N(t)$ visiting the city's zoo is given by the function\n\n$$\nN(t)=60+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(48-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time (in hours) since noon.  \n\nAt what time is the number of people in the zoo at a maximum, and how many people are at the zoo at that time?\n\nThe maximum number of people is\n{{field-1}}\nafter\n{{field-2}}\nhours." :question/worked-solution "To find when the number of people at the zoo is at maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(60+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(48-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(60)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({\\int }_{0}^{t}(48-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(48-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =48-3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=0$:\n\n$$\n48-3{t}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,t=4\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=4.$ To find out how many people are in the zoo when $t=4,$ we figure out $N(4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(4) & =60+{\\int }_{0}^{4}(48-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =60+{[48x-{x}^{3}]|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =60+[(48\\cdot 4-{4}^{3})-(0)] \\\\  & =60+[192-64] \\\\  & =60+128 \\\\  & =188\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $60<188$, we conclude that the maximum number of people at the zoo is $188$, and this will happen after $4$ hours."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506541 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147399 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3798ec35-19ef-559f-b8f8-cb0a0a03157b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Global Minimum or Maximum of a Function Given Its Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dfaac65-5999-5ebb-b5c5-894a0344cbaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505939}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0dfaac65-5999-5ebb-b5c5-894a0344cbaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505939} :answer-field/id #uuid "25f4130c-9394-541b-a643-051a656f347b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506396} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b09585d-43e7-56cb-8602-90e62261bd31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "130" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506184}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b09585d-43e7-56cb-8602-90e62261bd31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "130" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506184} :answer-field/id #uuid "265736ea-5f99-5ebe-9b6c-32c27324902a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506397}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "54f0409c-f2ef-5cc4-b4e4-ffc38eb896a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-349997" :question/problem "On a certain afternoon, the total number of people $N(t)$ visiting the city's zoo is given by the function\n\n$$\nN(t)=30+4{\\int }_{0}^{t}(10-2x)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time (in hours) since noon.  \n\nAt what time is the number of people in the zoo at a maximum, and how many people are at the zoo at that time?\n\nThe maximum number of people is\n{{field-1}}\nafter\n{{field-2}}\nhours." :question/worked-solution "To find when the number of people at the zoo is at maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(30+4{\\int }_{0}^{t}(10-2x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(30)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{\\int }_{0}^{t}(10-2x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(10-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4(10-2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=0$:\n\n$$\n4(10-2t)=0\\,⟹\\,t=5\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=5.$ To find out how many people are in the zoo when $t=5,$ we figure out $N(5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(5) & =30+4{\\int }_{0}^{5}(10-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =30+4{[10x-{x}^{2}]|}_{0}^{5} \\\\  & =30+4[(10\\cdot 5-{5}^{2})-(0)] \\\\  & =30+4\\cdot 25 \\\\  & =130\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $30<130$, we conclude that the maximum number of people at the zoo is $130$, and this will happen after $5$ hours."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332974 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147399 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3798ec35-19ef-559f-b8f8-cb0a0a03157b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Global Minimum or Maximum of a Function Given Its Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21c10ece-c009-56c5-be8d-90e6cb75497c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The minimum is $40$ participants  after $4$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330238} {:answer/id #uuid "4c7ffa2f-9e56-5efe-97c7-d12ccab36064" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The minimum is $32$ participants  after $0$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330645} {:answer/id #uuid "522f2b59-ef0a-5794-8023-90cf98db584b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The minimum is $8$ participants  after $4$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330698} {:answer/id #uuid "d7eb419f-421f-54a3-b0b8-0034e420363a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The minimum is $40$ participants  after $0$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331864} {:answer/id #uuid "f18e8e04-35b4-55b7-86fa-527938e47ab2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The minimum is $32$ participants  after $4$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332074}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "522f2b59-ef0a-5794-8023-90cf98db584b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The minimum is $8$ participants  after $4$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330698} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9450f60-3299-5ac4-9582-ce92545753e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332638}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "28fcc32f-48c3-5813-8388-8eda5e584ffe" :question/math-academy-id "q-62834" :question/problem "The total number of participants $S(t)$ registered for an online conference after $t$ days is given by the function\n\n$$\nS(t)=40+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(4x-16)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in days, since the conference was announced. \n\nAt what time is the number of participants at a minimum, and how many participants are registered at this time?" :question/worked-solution "To find when the number participants is minimized, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(40+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(4x-16)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(40)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({\\int }_{0}^{t}(4x-16)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(4x-16)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4t-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=0$:\n\n$$\n4x-16=0\\,⟹\\,t=4\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=4.$ To find out how many participants are registered when $t=4,$ we figure out $S(4)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(4) & =40+{\\int }_{0}^{4}(4x-16)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =40+{[2{x}^{2}-16x]|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =40+[(2(4)^{2}-16(4))-(0)] \\\\  & =40-32 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $8<40$, we conclude that the minimum number of participants is $8$, and this occurs after $4$ days."}]}
